<!--
    GH-8777: "PIC24: signed branch lifts to a raw SBORROW flag idiom."
    https://github.com/NationalSecurityAgency/ghidra/issues/8777

    The PIC24 spec models `bra ge` explicitly as the condition-flag identity
    N == V over the subtraction (V - K):
        ge = (SRL_N && SRL_OV) || (!SRL_N && !SRL_OV)          (== XNOR(N,V))
    where N = sign of (V - K)  and  V = INT_SBORROW(V, K)  (signed overflow).
    XNOR(N,V) is the textbook signed `V >= K` test, but upstream has no rule
    for it, so the raw flag algebra survives into the rendered loop condition:

      for (iVar1 = 0; bVar2 = 0 <= iVar1 + -7,
          (bVar2 || !SBORROW2(iVar1,7)) && (!bVar2 || SBORROW2(iVar1,7));
          iVar1 = iVar1 + 1) { ... }

    Here bVar2 = `0 <= iVar1 - 7` is NOT N (sign clear), so the tree is
    XNOR(!N, V) == N != V == `iVar1 < 7`: the loop continues while `ge` is
    false.  The machine code agrees: W2 counts 0..6, the body stores 0 to
    0x80 seven times, and `bra ge` leaves once W2 - 7 >= 0.

    Stage model: S3 "simplification-quiescence" (which algebraic/peephole rules
    fire).  kuna's RuleSborrowGe (group "analysis", always on) recognizes the
    XNOR(F, SBORROW(V,K)) tree in either De-Morgan dual form (AND-of-ORs or
    OR-of-ANDs) over a sign flag F of (V-K), and keeps its polarity:
      F = N   (`(V-K) s< 0`)                    ->  INT_SLESSEQUAL(K, V)  `K <= V`
      F = !N  (`0 s<= (V-K)`, `-1 s< (V-K)`)    ->  INT_SLESS(V, K)       `V < K`
    The loop condition is the clean signed comparison `v1 < 7` (seven
    iterations) and no SBORROW2 remains.  An earlier version read `0 <= x` as
    N and printed `7 <= v1`, a loop that never runs.

    Disassembly:
      0x000000 mov.w #0x0,W2
      0x000002 mov.w #0x7,W3
      0x000004 sub.w W2,W3,W12
      0x000006 bra   ge,0x10
      0x000008 mov.w #0x0,W0
      0x00000a mov.w W0,0x80
      0x00000c add.w W2,#0x1,W2
      0x00000e bra   0x4
      0x000010 return
-->
